    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. It uses a special fluid called refrigerant that flows through pipes. First, the refrigerant evaporates inside the fridge, absorbing heat from the food and air. That turns it into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes the gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils behind or beneath the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to absorb more heat. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, and your food stays fresh."
